    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey, im studying for my diesel mech exam in munich and i'm a bit stuck on turbochargers. can u explain how the exhaust gas actually turns the turbine and how that leads to more air getting into the engine? specifically how the pressure increases in the manifold?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Sure! In a turbocharger, exhaust gases from the engine flow into the turbine. These gases pass over the turbine blades, causing them to spin. The rotation of the turbine is connected through a shaft to a compressor wheel, which draws in and compresses additional air into the engine's intake manifold. As the compressed air flows into the engine, this increases the pressure, allowing the engine to take in more air, which results in better combustion and power output. This process is essentially how the exhaust gas energy is harnessed to boost engine performance.",
      "source": "target",
